  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Laccaria striatula is a small, delicate mushroom belonging to the Hydnangiaceae family, characterized by its thin flesh and distinctive gill structure. This species is commonly found in New England forests where it plays an important ecological role as a mycorrhizal fungus.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Striated Cap",
      "description": "The cap surface displays fine radial striations or grooves extending from the center to the margin, which is the defining characteristic referenced in its scientific name.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Gill Attachment",
      "description": "The gills are typically adnate to adnexed (attached directly or nearly so to the stem) and are relatively far apart compared to other small mushrooms.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Size and Coloration",
      "description": "This mushroom is notably small with a pale to tan or grayish cap, and the entire fruiting body is relatively thin and fragile.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Laccaria striatula thrives in moist forest environments throughout New England, commonly appearing in leaf litter and near decaying wood where it forms mycorrhizal associations with various tree species.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite its small and delicate appearance, Laccaria striatula is a mycorrhizal partner with trees, meaning it forms a mutually beneficial relationship with tree roots to help them absorb water and nutrients from the soil.",
